{"summary":"Reject octal and hexadecimal escapes whose values exceed one byte.","explanation":"`Unescape.BasicFunction` checks each entry in `bad`, including `"\\777"` and `"\\xABCD"`. For those inputs, `CUnescape(e, &out, &error)` returns true and leaves `error.empty()` true, though the test expects false and a nonempty error; the no-error overload also returns true instead of false. Both byte-range checks were incorrectly conditional on `leave_nulls_escaped`, which is false for `CUnescape`. Remove that condition. Since hexadecimal escapes may contain arbitrarily many digits, accumulate only while the value fits in a byte and remember overflow while consuming the rest of the escape; this prevents unsigned wraparound from making an oversized value appear valid.","edits":[{"path":"absl/strings/escaping.cc","old":"          if (ch > 0xff && leave_nulls_escaped) {","new":"          if (ch > 0xff) {"},{"path":"absl/strings/escaping.cc","old":"          unsigned int ch = 0;
          auto hex_start = p;
          while (p + 1 < src.size() &&
                 absl::ascii_isxdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(src[p + 1]))) {
            // Arbitrarily many hex digits
            ch = (ch << 4) + hex_digit_to_int(src[++p]);
          }
          if (ch > 0xFF && leave_nulls_escaped) {","new":"          unsigned int ch = 0;
          bool exceeds_byte = false;
          auto hex_start = p;
          while (p + 1 < src.size() &&
                 absl::ascii_isxdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(src[p + 1]))) {
            // Arbitrarily many hex digits
            const unsigned int digit = hex_digit_to_int(src[++p]);
            if (ch > 0x0F) {
              exceeds_byte = true;
            } else if (!exceeds_byte) {
              ch = (ch << 4) + digit;
            }
          }
          if (exceeds_byte) {"}]}
